
[[exposing-native-resources]]
=== Exposing native resources

A module's `package.yaml` can link against shared libraries through its
`cpp-deps` and `cxx-flags` fields (<<configuring-cpp-build>>). The `expose` field handles a
different need: a module that defines a {cpp} struct, a
Python class, or an R helper whose definition downstream foreign code
needs to `#include` (or `import`, or `source`) by name. On install,
listed files are copied to per-language well-known paths under
`$MORLOC_HOME`, namespaced by module name. Downstream code then refers
to them through a stable path that is the same for every consumer.

[source,yaml]
----
expose:
  cpp: [person.hpp]
  py:  [__init__.py, helpers/]
  r:   [util.R]
----

Each key is optional. Paths are relative to the module root; glob
patterns (`*` within a segment, `**` across segments, trailing `/` for
a directory) follow the same syntax as `include`. Subtree structure is
preserved on copy -- essential for Python packages with `__init__.py`
markers and for {cpp} headers that `#include` siblings by relative path.

[cols="1,3,3", options="header"]
|===
| Language | Destination | Consumer code
| {cpp}    | `+$MORLOC_STATE/modules/include/<lib>/<module>/...+` | `+#include "<lib>/<module>/foo.hpp"+`
| Python   | `+$MORLOC_STATE/modules/python/mlc_lib/<lib>/<py_module>/...+` | `+import mlc_lib.<lib>.<py_module>.foo+`
| R        | `+$MORLOC_STATE/modules/R/<lib>/<module>/...+` | `+.morloc.source("<lib>/<module>/foo.R")+`
|===

`<lib>` is the module's library: `std` for the standard library
(`#include "std/tensor-cpp/tensor.hpp"`). For Python, hyphens in the
library and module names are converted to underscores so the destination
is a legal Python identifier (a module `tensor-cpp` becomes `tensor_cpp`).
The {cpp} subtree `$MORLOC_STATE/modules/include` is
already on every {cpp} pool's `-I` path, so no compile flags need
tweaking; consumers write the namespaced `#include`. `morloc
uninstall` symmetrically removes the exposed copies alongside the
install dir.

A worked example. The `people` module declares a Morloc type backed by
a {cpp} struct and exposes the header that defines it:

[source,morloc]
.people/main.loc
----
module people (Person, makePerson)

import root-cpp

record Person where
  name :: Str
  age :: Int
record Cpp => Person = "person_t"

source Cpp from "person.hpp" ("make_person" as makePerson)

makePerson :: Str -> Int -> Person
----

[source,yaml]
.people/package.yaml
----
name: people
library: weena
version: 0.1.0
expose:
  cpp: [person.hpp]
----

[source,cpp]
.people/person.hpp
----
#ifndef PEOPLE_PERSON_HPP
#define PEOPLE_PERSON_HPP

#include <string>

struct person_t {
    std::string name;
    int age;
};

inline person_t make_person(const std::string& name, int age) {
    return person_t{name, age};
}

#endif
----

Install with `morloc install ./people`. The exposed header now lives at
`$MORLOC_STATE/modules/include/weena/people/person.hpp`, under the module's
library and name. A downstream program imports
the Morloc type and uses the underlying {cpp} struct directly in its own
foreign code:

[source,morloc]
.main.loc
----
module main (greeting)

import weena/people (Person, makePerson)
import root-cpp

source Cpp from "src.hpp" ("greet")

greet :: Person -> Str

greeting :: Str
greeting = greet (makePerson "Alice" 30)
----

[source,cpp]
.src.hpp
----
#include "weena/people/person.hpp"
#include <string>

inline std::string greet(const person_t& p) {
    return "Hello, " + p.name + "! Age " + std::to_string(p.age);
}
----

The same exposed header is discoverable from non-Morloc {cpp} programs
too: compile with `g++ -I$MORLOC_STATE/modules/include` and `#include
"weena/people/person.hpp"` works identically.


